### Potential Issues & Considerations

1.  **Duplicate Values**: The current implementation includes duplicate values in the BST. In some definitions of a BST, duplicates are either disallowed or must be handled specifically (e.g., always placed in the right subtree). The current recursive "middle-element" approach naturally places duplicates in either the left or right subtree depending on their position relative to the "middle" index, which still maintains the BST property ($Left \le Root \le Right$).
2.  **Performance Bottleneck**: Selection Sort is $O(n^2)$. While this was specifically requested, it will be very slow for arrays larger than 10,000–20,000 elements.
