Because the `real()` function was applied after the inverse FFT, the spikes added to the frequency domain were effectively mirrored to their conjugate symmetric positions. To restore the image, you must identify these spikes in the Fourier transform of `y` and "clean" them, typically by interpolating the values from neighboring pixels.

