2.  **Handling the `real()` operator**: In the degradation code, `y = real(ifft2(x))`. Taking the real part of an inverse FFT is mathematically equivalent to ensuring the frequency spectrum is conjugate symmetric. This means the noise added at `(10, 10)` also appears at the symmetric location `(end-10+2, end-10+2)`. My code calculates these symmetric coordinates and cleans them as well.
